  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 152 PM MST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nWarm and dry weather is expected to continue for the rest of the \noperational forecast period. Picking up on Tuesday, we will have a \nlittle bit to talk about as a clipper system passes just to our \nnortheast. Models are in good agreement keeping the system to our \nnorth meaning nearly all ensembles are totally dry for the forecast \narea. On the south side of the upper level shortwave, we can expect \nto see 700-mb winds creep up Tuesday morning. A surface cyclone will \nlead the upper level low developing over the Black Hills region \nTuesday morning. By the afternoon hours, expect this feature to pull \nto the east, dragging the surface trough eastward away from the \nLaramie range, and thus pulling gusty west to northwest winds across \nthe area into the High Plains. Most areas can expect maximum gusts \nin the 35 to 45 mph range, but there is a chance for high winds in \nthe typical wind prone areas. Craig to Casper 700 and 850-mb height \ngradients are around 45 to 50 meters, which is just on the low side \nfor high wind events. In house guidance now gives each of the wind \nprone areas about a 40% chance for high winds, so this will be one \nto watch. The best window is late Tuesday morning through about \nsunset. Very dry air will also mix through the atmosphere, producing \nat least elevated fire weather conditions. Despite modest cool air \nadvection through the day, we should still see temperatures climb to \nabout 10F above seasonal averages, generally in the 60s for the \nplains and mid to upper 50s in the valleys west of the Laramie \nrange. \n\nWednesday will remain breezy, but gusts should be more in the 25 to \n35 mph range. Northwest flow will persist aloft behind the departing \nshortwave trough. 700-mb temperatures in the 0 to +2C range will \nsupport highs closer to average, but still on the warm side. \nWednesday will be the coolest day of the week, with upper 50s to \nlower 60s anticipated for the plains, and mid 50s for the valleys \nwest of the Laramie range. \n\nA strong ridge amplifying to our west will encroach on the area \nThursday into the weekend. Expect a gradual warming trend Thursday \nand Friday, with 700-mb temperatures peaking around +5 to +7C \nFriday. High temperatures will remain about 10 to 15F above seasonal \naverages. The ridge is expected to flatten out a bit over the \nweekend. 700-mb temperatures drop slightly, and thus daytime high \nexpectations are a few degrees cooler than Friday for Saturday and \nSunday, but still warm for this time of year. Overall, it should be \na pleasant weekend. Portions of Carbon, Converse, and Albany \ncounties could be breezy, but nothing remotely unusual for this time \nof year. With little to no precipitation expected anywhere in the \narea for the next 7 days, it is tempting to look beyond the \noperational forecast period for some hints at a pattern change. \nEnsembles continue to kick the can down the road and push the \npotential change further out, but most are showing the longwave \nPacific trough moving inland into the western CONUS by the weekend. \nIf this occurs, the door to potential storminess may open again in \nthe November 16 to 19 period. About 60% of ensembles show a storm \nsystem passing through our area during this period, while 40% remain \nmostly dry.",
